How to make a BlueJ release
---------------------------

NOTE: This process changed when the build moved to Gradle and the installers
started being built by GitHub Actions. Building locally with Ant is no longer
how releases are made; see HOWTO.make-mac-release for Mac-specific signing
notes only.

PART I - BEFORE BUILDING
-------------------------

 0. Before building, check the copyright years (for display in the copyright dialog), in the
    showCopyright() method in PkgMgrFrame.

 1. Set the right BlueJ version number in version.properties (repo root):
       bluej_major / bluej_minor / bluej_release / bluej_suffix / bluej_rcnumber
    If you bump to a new x.y.z (or change bluej_suffix), add a new GUID for it in
    version.properties (further down, in the "BlueJ GUIDs" section) - get one from
    guidgen.com. The comment at the top of version.properties explains this.

 2. Commit and push the version.properties change (and any copyright-year change) to main.


PART II - BUILDING THE RELEASE CANDIDATE (GITHUB ACTIONS)
-----------------------------------------------------------

 3. Pushing a change to version.properties automatically triggers the "Build Installers"
    workflow (.github/workflows/build-installers.yml). You can also trigger it manually from
    the Actions tab on GitHub ("Build Installers" -> "Run workflow") if you need to rebuild
    without changing version.properties.

    This workflow builds, via Gradle, installers for:
      - Windows (msi + zip)
      - Ubuntu/Debian, x64 and arm64 (deb + generic jar)
      - Mac, Intel and Apple Silicon (dmg, signed and notarized using the secrets configured
        in the repo)
    There is no more manual "ant windows" / "ant mac" / "fakeroot ant debian" step - all
    platforms are built in parallel on GitHub-hosted (and one self-hosted ARM64) runners.

 4. When the workflow run completes, download the build artifacts from the run's summary
    page on GitHub (bluej-installers-windows, bluej-installers-other, bluej-installers-mac-*,
    bluej-installers-arm64, and the equivalent greenfoot-installers-* ones). Each is a zip
    containing the actual installer files.

 5. Test the installers from these artifacts on each platform. If you find problems, fix them,
    bump bluej_rcnumber in version.properties, push, and repeat from step 3.


PART III - RELEASING BLUEJ ON GITHUB
--------------------------------------

 6. When you're happy with a build, do a final version.properties change to drop the RC
    suffix (i.e. this is now the real release, not a candidate), commit and push.

 7. Tag the released commit in git as BLUEJ-RELEASE-x.y.z (e.g. BLUEJ-RELEASE-6.0.1) and push
    the tag. Pushing a tag matching BLUEJ-RELEASE-* (or GREENFOOT-RELEASE-* for Greenfoot)
    triggers the same "Build Installers" workflow, and this time its final job,
    "Publish-Release", automatically:
      - collects the built installers for the matching product (bluej-installers-* for a
        BLUEJ-RELEASE-* tag; greenfoot-installers-* for GREENFOOT-RELEASE-*), already named
        with version and architecture (the Linux job renames the .deb to add "-x64-"/
        "-arm64-", since the Ant build itself doesn't distinguish Linux architectures in the
        filename)
      - creates a DRAFT GitHub Release for that tag with all of those files attached.
    No manual downloading/renaming/re-uploading of artifacts is needed any more.

 8. Go to the repository's Releases page, open the new draft, write the release notes (see
    previous releases for the style used), double-check the attached files, and click
    "Publish release" (leave "pre-release" unchecked unless it genuinely is one).
    There is no separate source-zip upload; source is available at the tag itself. There is
    also no more manual copying of files to a bluej.org/www.bluej.org download server -
    GitHub Releases is the download location.

 9. Update the BlueJ web site to point at (or announce) the new GitHub release:
     - update the front page (index.html)
        - change version, date, and download links
     - update the versions page (versions.html)
        - add section with version, date, changes and download links
     - update the current version information (version.info)
        - change the version number and the release notes' summary.

10. In the bugs database (http://bugs.bluej.org/trac/bluej),

   1. Add a future release milestone ("Roadmap", "New Milestone").

   2. Mark the new release milestone as completed.  Use the option that
      Trac provides to retarget open bugs to the future milestone.
      ("Roadmap" -> click on milestone -> "Edit milestone";
       do not go through the Admin panel!).

   3. Also check that there are no bugs against the current release which are marked as
      FIXED but for which the milestone isn't set. Set the milestone to the new release
      for such bugs.

11. Also in the bugs database, add the new version so that bugs can be reported against
    it.

12. Update the support form at https://www.bluej.org/support.html - the drop-down
    version field should have the new version added as an option.

13. Send a mail message to bluej-announce@bluej.org, announcing the release.

14. More public announcements. See HOWTO.announce-release.
